statement:
Let $k$ be an algebraically closed field and $C$ a smooth connected curve over $k$ [def-sc-curves] (nonempty, connected, of dimension one, separated, of finite type and smooth over $k$). Then:

1. $C$ is a Noetherian integral scheme [def-pc-integral-scheme]; let $\eta$ be its generic point [def-sc-generic-point], so that $k(C)=\mathcal O_{C,\eta}$ is a field [def-sc-function-field] and, for every nonempty open $V\subseteq C$ and every $x\in V$, the maps $\mathcal O_C(V)\to\mathcal O_{C,x}\to\mathcal O_{C,\eta}=k(C)$ (germ at $x$, then the map of stalks sending the germ at $x$ of a section to its germ at $\eta$) are injective;
2. every point of $C$ other than $\eta$ is a closed point [def-weil-closed-points], $\eta$ is not a closed point, and $C$ has at least one closed point;
3. for every closed point $P$ of $C$ the local ring $\mathcal O_{C,P}$ is a discrete valuation ring [def-sc-discrete-valuation-ring] whose fraction field is $k(C)$ (via the injection of 1.); if $t$ is a uniformizer of $\mathcal O_{C,P}$, every $f\in k(C)^*$ can be written $f=ut^n$ with $u$ a unit of $\mathcal O_{C,P}$ and $n\in\mathbf Z$, both uniquely determined, and $n$ does not depend on the choice of $t$; we write $v_P(f)=n$, so that $v_P:k(C)^*\to\mathbf Z$ is a surjective group homomorphism (the normalized valuation of $\mathcal O_{C,P}$) and $\mathcal O_{C,P}\setminus\{0\}=\{f\in k(C)^*:v_P(f)\ge0\}$.
